{"availability":"available","full":"ericleib/ngx-remark","name":"ngx-remark","description":"Render markdown with custom Angular templates","category":"Productivity","language":"TypeScript","stars":23,"forks":5,"created":"2023-07-18T08:28:19Z","updated":"2026-05-15T08:05:06Z","license":"MIT","branch":"master","homepage":"https://ericleib.github.io/ngx-remark/","topics":[],"demo":"https://ericleib.github.io/ngx-remark/","color":"#323057","ink":"#d6caff","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-18T21:02:05.961Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-18T21:02:05.961Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-18T21:02:05.961Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-09T13:20:05.659Z","stars":22,"forks":5},{"at":"2026-10-12T13:36:33.380Z","stars":23,"forks":5},{"at":"2026-10-18T21:02:05.961Z","stars":23,"forks":5}],"technologies":[],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["ngx-remark is a lightweight library that renders Markdown using native Angular components and templates.","Most Markdown libraries for Angular convert the source to HTML and then bind it with [innerHTML]. The problem with this approach is that the resulting HTML lives outside Angular's component tree — you cannot use Angular components, directives, pipes, or dependency injection inside it.","ngx-remark takes a different route. It uses Remark to parse Markdown into an AST, then renders that tree with real Angular templates. The component ships with clean default templates for every standard Markdown element, but you can override any of them with your own components."],"features":["Rendering code blocks with a full-featured editor (e.g. Monaco, CodeMirror)","Adding interactive tooltips, popovers, or badges on specific elements","Turning links or buttons into Angular-powered actions (routing, modals, etc.)","Deep integration with Angular features like the Router, forms, or signals"],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/ericleib/ngx-remark/blob/master/README.md","fingerprint":"d4b0f801d0bf232220d99d76583f7d752c876e063e1924259e21ee25dcdff01e","extractedAt